INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('020115b7f78d15e08fd1','AMEDEX 2025 OBS  (1).pdf','10','Obstetrics/Pharmacology','Which of the following medications, if taken during pregnancy, could have caused the facial anomalies shown in the accompanying photograph?',NULL,'B','Option B Is correct

The apparent features in the photograph are a
broad nasal bridge, a short upturned nose, a long
upper lip, and a shallow philtrum. These features
are characteristics of fetal valproate syndrome.

Fetal valproate syndrome presents with the
following facial features:

e Hypotelorism (eyes abnormally closer to
each other)

e Maxillary hypoplasia

¢ Small upturned nose

e Long upper lip with shallow philtrum

e Low-set ears

¢ Small mouth
Other anomalies may include:

¢ Congenital heart defects

e Musculoskeletal abnormalities, such as club
hands and inguinal hernias

e Developmental delay, especially in language
and communication

e Neural tube defects, such as spina bifida

Previously, women with epilepsy were informed
that their risk for fetal malformations was increased
2- to 3-fold, but more recent data suggest that the

risk may not be as significant as once thought,

yarticularly for newer agents. More recent stu
showed that the risk of fetal malformation follo

e Neural tube defects, such as spina bifida

Previously, women with epilepsy were informed
that their risk for fetal malformations was increased
2- to 3-fold, but more recent data suggest that the
risk may not be as significant as once thought,
particularly for newer agents. More recent studies
showed that the risk of fetal malformation following
intrauterine exposure to anticonvulsants that are
currently in use is approximately the same or only
slightly elevated compared to children without
exposure. Sodium valproate is an important
exception to these findings. Women treated with
sodium valproate are at significantly increased risk
of fetal malformations. One study showed that
major malformations occurred in 9% of fetuses with
first-trimester valproate exposure; however,
different data suggest the rate to be as high as
16%.

Reference(s) ° PubMed - Teratogenic effects of
antiepileptic drugs

- PubMed - Fetal valproate syndro
me

Hide the correct option

